.Dd October 11, 2026
.Dt DRMADM 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm drmadm
.Nd show and select the DRM driver stack
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl R Ar root
.Op Cm status
.Nm
.Op Fl R Ar root
.Cm select
.Cm legacy | new
.Nm
.Op Fl R Ar root
.Cm perms
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Two Direct Rendering Manager (DRM) driver stacks can be installed on the
same system:
.Bl -tag -width "legacy"
.It Cm legacy
the stack of the package
.Sy driver/graphics/drm :
the core module
.Sy misc/drm
and the Intel driver
.Xr i915 7D .
.It Cm new
the illumos-drm stack: the core module
.Sy misc/drm_core
.Pq package Sy driver/graphics/drm-core
and the drivers that depend on it, among them
.Sy virtio_gpu ,
and the Intel driver
.Sy i915n .
.El
.Pp
Both stacks create their device nodes with the node type
.Dv DDI_NT_DISPLAY_DRM ,
from which
.Xr devfsadm 8
makes the
.Pa /dev/dri
links through the link module of
.Sy driver/graphics/drm-devlink .
.Pp
The two stacks must never be loaded at the same time.
Each core refuses to load, with
.Er EBUSY
and a warning on the console, while the other core is loaded, and every
driver depends on its core, so a driver of one stack cannot load while the
other stack is loaded.
.Nm
decides which stack the system loads at boot and which driver binds an
Intel or virtio GPU.
.Pp
Installing a package of either stack selects nothing.
Without a selection nothing is excluded: the aliases that
.Sy driver/graphics/drm
installs for
.Sy i915
make the legacy stack drive an Intel GPU, and whichever core loads first
keeps the other one out.
The packages of the new stack bind no Intel or virtio GPU:
.Sy i915n
is delivered without aliases, and
.Nm
moves the aliases of the Intel GPUs from
.Sy i915
to it.
.Sy virtio_gpu
also ships without driver-action aliases; its package delivers the alias
list as data in
.Pa /usr/share/drmadm/virtio-gpu.aliases .
.Ss Selecting a stack
.Nm Cm select Ar stack
does the following and prints each change:
.Bl -enum
.It
It writes the fragment
.Pa /etc/system.d/driver:graphics:drmadm ,
which records the selection and the alias set and excludes the modules of
the other stack, each as
.Ar class Ns / Ns Ar module
and as the bare module name, so that a load by an explicit path is
refused too
.Pq see Xr system 5 .
For the new stack these are
.Sy misc/drm ,
.Sy drv/i915
and
.Sy drv/radeon ;
for the legacy stack
.Sy misc/drm_core ,
.Sy drv/i915n ,
.Sy drv/virtio_gpu ,
.Sy drv/cdrmnull
and
.Sy drv/rdrmnull .
A driver of the new stack that is not in this list is still kept from
loading by the exclusion of
.Sy misc/drm_core .
The fragment is written first, so that a failure in the next step loses
no alias.
.It
It moves the Intel GPU aliases to the driver of the selected stack with
.Xr update_drv 8
.Fl n ,
which edits
.Pa /etc/driver_aliases
without attaching a driver, and checks the result; if a move fails, it
gives the aliases back and restores the previous fragment.
The aliases are those the installed
.Sy driver/graphics/drm
declares for
.Sy i915 ,
those bound to
.Sy i915
or
.Sy i915n ,
and those
.Nm
recorded before. For new it also includes the admitted GPU IDs from
.Pa /usr/share/drmadm/i915n.aliases
when delivered by the installed i915n package. This covers devices such as
Skylake that the legacy driver does not list. Selection records that set
separately; selecting legacy removes new-only bindings instead of handing
them to the old driver. Older i915n packages without this file retain the
legacy-derived behavior. After upgrading, run select new again in the test
boot environment and reboot to add these bindings.
When the driver of the selected stack is not installed, the aliases stay
unbound, and an Intel GPU falls back to the generic VGA console driver,
.Sy vgatext .
Aliases of these devices bound to any other driver are left alone and
reported.
.Pp
For virtio_gpu it binds the aliases from
.Pa /usr/share/drmadm/virtio-gpu.aliases
(one PCI alias per line, blank lines and comments beginning with # allowed)
only for new and only with the virtio-gpu package and driver installed.
For legacy it removes them. The fragment records this separate alias set
before any moves; rollback restores both Intel and virtio bindings.
.It
It runs
.Nm bootadm Cm update-archive ,
since the kernel reads both files from the boot archive.
.El
.Pp
The selection takes effect at the next boot.
After that boot, run
.Nm devfsadm Fl C
once: it removes the
.Pa /dev/dri
links of the devices of the stack that is no longer loaded.
.Nm Cm select
only edits files read at boot: loaded modules of either stack stay as they
are until the next boot (a display driver that is the system console cannot
be unloaded).
It also refuses while another
.Nm
runs.
Selecting the stack that is already selected changes nothing.
.Ss Xorg configuration
The drmadm package delivers a generic modesetting template for
.Pa /dev/dri/card0
at
.Pa /usr/share/drmadm/20-modesetting.conf ,
outside Xorg's search paths. The boot service
.Sy svc:/system/graphics/drm-perms
runs
.Nm Cm perms
before the optional display managers lightdm, gdm, cde-login and xdm.
It installs
.Pa /etc/X11/xorg.conf.d/20-drmadm.conf
only if new is selected and in effect since this boot (the recorded
selection time precedes the kernel boot time). Otherwise it removes its
own active copy. A selection pending reboot does not enable the template.
.Nm Cm select
does not change the running Xorg configuration.
.Pp
The saved copy
.Pa /etc/X11/.drmadm.conf
records the exact bytes installed. An unowned file, a changed file or a
symlink at the active path is left alone, including across template
updates. The old unconditional virtio-gpu configuration is no longer
packaged; IPS removes the old packaged copy on upgrade.
.Pp
For the new stack use virtio-gpu-pci with a separate std VGA console
(QEMU
.Sy -vga std -device virtio-gpu-pci ) ,
not virtio-vga. Binding virtio-vga with the new stack still loses the
console: virtio_gpu has no console takeover like i915n. The legacy default
leaves virtio-vga's stock VGA console intact.
.Ss Device node permissions
The packages of the new stack give the card and render nodes of their
drivers the permissions
.Sy 0666 root sys
in
.Pa /etc/minor_perm :
any process may open a render node, and the DRM core decides which file
of a card node may change the display (the DRM master).
The rule
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/dev/vt/console_user  0600  /dev/dri/*
.Ed
.Pp
of
.Pa /etc/logindevperm
.Pq see Xr logindevperm 5
gives every
.Pa /dev/dri
node to the user logged in on the console and back to root with mode
0600 at the logout.
.Xr ttymon 8
of
.Sy svc:/system/console-login
applies the logout half each time it opens the console, at every boot
and after every console logout, so the nodes of every DRM driver attached
by then become
.Sy 0600 root root .
.Xr devfs 4FS
keeps a changed owner, group or mode of a device node in a shadow node on
the root file system, which takes precedence over
.Pa /etc/minor_perm
from then on, also after a reboot or a reload of the driver.
.Pp
.Nm Cm perms
undoes this for the new stack:
.Bl -enum
.It
It restricts each rule of
.Pa /etc/logindevperm
with a device under
.Pa /dev/dri
and no driver list to the drivers of the legacy stack: the
.Pa /dev/dri
devices move to a rule of their own with
.Sy driver=i915,radeon ,
after a comment line that says so, and the other devices of the rule stay
where they were.
For the legacy drivers nothing changes; the nodes of the new stack's
drivers are no longer given to the console user.
Rules with a driver list are left as they are, and reported if they name
a driver of the new stack; so are rules that reach
.Pa /dev/dri
only through a pattern, such as
.Pa /dev/*/* ,
which are reported as applying to every driver.
If a rewritten rule would be longer than the 254 bytes per line that
.Xr logindevperm 5
is read with,
.Nm
changes nothing and fails.
The file is replaced with
.Xr rename 2 ;
running
.Nm Cm perms
again changes nothing.
.It
On the running system, it resets every
.Pa /dev/dri
node of a driver of the new stack
.Pq Sy i915n , virtio_gpu , cdrmnull , rdrmnull
whose owner, group or mode differs from its
.Pa /etc/minor_perm
entry to that entry, and reports each node it resets.
The entry is the one the kernel applies: of two different patterns of the
driver that match the minor name the later in the file, else the
.Sy *
entry.
The nodes of a driver that a rule of
.Pa /etc/logindevperm
names are left alone, and so are those of drivers that are not loaded:
.Nm
finds a node's driver in
.Pa /etc/path_to_inst ,
or for a pseudo device from its node name,
and does not look the node up otherwise
.Pq a node for whose path several loaded drivers are listed is reported and skipped, since a lookup would load the
driver.
.El
.Pp
The service
.Sy svc:/system/graphics/drm-perms ,
which
.Sy driver/graphics/drmadm
delivers, runs
.Nm Cm perms
at every boot after
.Sy svc:/system/device/local
and before
.Sy svc:/system/console-login ,
and when the package is installed or updated. It also reconciles Xorg
configuration as described above.
To give a new-stack driver's nodes other permissions, change its
.Pa /etc/minor_perm
entry with
.Nm update_drv Fl m
and run
.Nm Cm perms ;
the service resets a node changed with
.Xr chmod 1
or
.Xr chown 1
at the next boot.
To keep the console-user assignment for these nodes, disable the service
and remove the driver list from the rule.
.Pp
.Pa /etc/logindevperm
is an editable file of the package
.Sy SUNWcs :
while it is edited, an update of that package does not replace it.
To take the packaged version, run
.Nm pkg Cm revert Pa /etc/logindevperm ;
the next boot, or
.Nm Cm perms ,
restricts its rule again.
.Ss Status
.Nm Cm status ,
or
.Nm
without a subcommand,
prints the selected stack and whether it is in effect since the last boot
or from the next one, the installed packages of both stacks, the loaded
modules of both stacks, how many Intel GPU aliases are bound to
.Sy i915
and to
.Sy i915n
and how many are unbound, the virtio_gpu alias counts, the number of
.Pa /dev/dri
links, the
.Pa /etc/logindevperm
rules for
.Pa /dev/dri
and, on the running system, the mode, owner and group of each
.Pa /dev/dri
node of a loaded driver with its
.Pa /etc/minor_perm
entry where they differ, and every
.Sy exclude
line of
.Pa /etc/system
and
.Pa /etc/system.d
that names a module of either stack.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl R Ar root
Operate on the system image at
.Ar root ,
such as a boot environment mounted with
.Nm beadm Cm mount ,
instead of the running system.
The check for loaded modules is skipped,
.Xr update_drv 8
runs with
.Fl b Ar root
and the boot archive of
.Ar root
is updated.
.Cm perms
edits only the
.Pa /etc/logindevperm
of
.Ar root ;
its nodes are reset and its Xorg configuration is reconciled by the
service when it boots.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/system.d/driver:graphics:drmadm
The selection and its excludes, written only by
.Nm .
.It Pa /etc/driver_aliases
The alias bindings that
.Nm
changes through
.Xr update_drv 8 .
.It Pa /etc/logindevperm
The console-user rules whose
.Pa /dev/dri
entries
.Nm Cm perms
restricts to the legacy drivers.
.It Pa /etc/minor_perm
The permissions of the device nodes, which
.Nm Cm perms
restores.
.It Pa /usr/share/drmadm/virtio-gpu.aliases
Opt-in aliases owned by driver/graphics/virtio-gpu.
.It Pa /usr/share/drmadm/20-modesetting.conf
Inactive generic Xorg template owned by driver/graphics/drmadm.
.It Pa /etc/X11/xorg.conf.d/20-drmadm.conf
Active configuration installed by drmadm for the new stack at boot.
.It Pa /etc/X11/.drmadm.conf
Exact saved copy used to preserve administrator files and edits.
.It Pa /lib/svc/manifest/system/drm-perms.xml
The service
.Sy svc:/system/graphics/drm-perms .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
The exit status is 2 for a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Example 1 Selecting the new stack for development
.Bd -literal
# drmadm status
# drmadm select new
# reboot
\&...
# devfsadm -C
.Ed
.Ss Example 2 Recovering from a selection that does not boot to a display
Boot the previous boot environment, or boot single user and remove the
fragment:
.Bd -literal
# rm /etc/system.d/driver:graphics:drmadm
# bootadm update-archive
.Ed
.Pp
The Intel GPU aliases then still belong to the driver of the removed
selection; run
.Nm Cm select Cm legacy
once the system is up.
From another boot environment the selection of a broken one can be
changed directly:
.Bd -literal
# beadm mount broken /mnt
# drmadm -R /mnt select legacy
# beadm umount broken
.Ed
.Sh INTERFACE STABILITY
.Sy Uncommitted
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chmod 1 ,
.Xr chown 1 ,
.Xr pkg 1 ,
.Xr rename 2 ,
.Xr devfs 4FS ,
.Xr logindevperm 5 ,
.Xr system 5 ,
.Xr i915 7D ,
.Xr beadm 8 ,
.Xr bootadm 8 ,
.Xr devfsadm 8 ,
.Xr modinfo 8 ,
.Xr modunload 8 ,
.Xr ttymon 8 ,
.Xr update_drv 8
.Sh NOTES
While the new stack is selected,
.Nm pkg Cm verify Sy driver/graphics/drm
reports the aliases of
.Sy i915
as missing, and
.Nm pkg Cm fix
would give them back to
.Sy i915 ;
run
.Nm Cm select Cm new
again afterwards, and after a
.Nm pkg Cm update
of
.Sy driver/graphics/drm
that adds aliases to
.Sy i915 .
